{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyjqZvECErCZzPQ76eBv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No, I'd stop tuning. Three detectors that are all still noisy usually means the method is wrong, not the thresholds. The pixel approach is trying to work backwards to geometry your generator already knows.\n\n**Why the pixel approach stays noisy**\n- Anti-aliasing, JPEG/PNG compression and subpixel rendering blur every edge, so any threshold is a tradeoff between false positives and misses.\n- Figures with white or transparent margins look the same as empty slide space, so a real clip can be invisible.\n- Backgrounds, gradients and themes change what counts as \"content,\" so a threshold tuned on one deck breaks on the next.\n- A clipped figure often looks fine. Content just ends at the edge, and nothing in the pixels says \"this was cut.\"\n\n**What to do instead: check the geometry in the generator**\n\nYour code computes every figure's position and size, so check those numbers at placement time:\n\n```python\nSLIDE_W, SLIDE_H = 13.333, 7.5   # inches (or EMU); use your real dimensions\nSAFE = 0.25                      # margin you consider \"too close to the edge\"\n\ndef check_placement(name, x, y, w, h, slide_no):\n    problems = []\n    if x < SAFE or y < SAFE:\n        problems.append(\"outside safe area (top/left)\")\n    if x + w > SLIDE_W - SAFE or y + h > SLIDE_H - SAFE:\n        problems.append(\"outside safe area (bottom/right)\")\n    if w <= 0 or h <= 0:\n        problems.append(\"degenerate size\")\n    for p in problems:\n        print(f\"slide {slide_no}: {name}: {p}  box=({x:.2f},{y:.2f},{w:.2f},{h:.2f})\")\n    return not problems\n```\n\nCall it wherever a figure gets placed, collect every box per slide, and also check:\n1. **Overlaps:** pairwise intersection of boxes on the same slide, minus any overlaps you meant to have.\n2. **Aspect ratio:** compare the source image's ratio with the placed w/h. Distortion and crop-to-fit are a quieter kind of clipping.\n3. **Text:** measure each string with the real font (e.g. `PIL.ImageFont.truetype(...).getbbox(text)`, plus line wrapping) and compare it to its text box. This is the one place where the geometry you set isn't the geometry that renders.\n\nThese checks are exact, so a slide either passes or fails, and they run in milliseconds without rendering anything.\n\n**Where pixels still help**\n\nKeep a small render-based backstop for things the generator can't see, like font substitution in the renderer or autofit behavior. Make it a different kind of test:\n- **Golden-image diffs:** render once, approve it by eye, then flag any slide whose render changes beyond a tolerance. That catches regressions without trying to define \"clipped.\"\n- **Edge-strip check:** look only at the outer few pixels of each slide for non-background content. It's a much narrower question than general clipping detection, so it's far less noisy.\n\nIf you share how the generator places figures (python-pptx, a LaTeX/Beamer pipeline, HTML-to-PDF, etc.), I can wire the geometry checks into it directly."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12150,"cache_read_input_tokens":25083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12150},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1","uuid":"bf830486-4095-46c1-808e-a26fa93a0e36","timestamp":"2026-10-13T06:10:42.465Z","request_id":"req_011CfyjqZiKootSa8JQjqG2V"}
